AbstractDict{K, V}

Supertype for dictionary-like types with keys of type K and values of type
V. Dict, IdDict and other types are subtypes of this. An AbstractDict{K, V}
should be an iterator of Pair{K, V}.

AbstractMatrix{T}

Supertype for two-dimensional arrays (or array-like types) with elements of
type T. Alias for AbstractArray{T,2}.

AbstractSet{T}

Supertype for set-like types whose elements are of type T. Set, BitSet and
other types are subtypes of this.

AbstractUnitRange{T} <: OrdinalRange{T, T}

Supertype for ranges with a step size of oneunit(T) with elements of type T.
UnitRange and other types are subtypes of this.

AbstractVector{T}

Supertype for one-dimensional arrays (or array-like types) with elements of
type T. Alias for AbstractArray{T,1}.

DenseArray{T, N} <: AbstractArray{T,N}

N-dimensional dense array with elements of type T. The elements of a dense
array are stored contiguously in memory.

DenseMatrix{T}

Two-dimensional DenseArray with elements of type T. Alias for
DenseArray{T,2}.

DenseVector{T}

One-dimensional DenseArray with elements of type T. Alias for
DenseArray{T,1}.

Base.Pairs(values, keys) <: AbstractDict{eltype(keys), eltype(values)}

Transforms an indexable container into a Dictionary-view of the same data.
Modifying the key-space of the underlying data may invalidate this object.

VecOrMat{T}

Union type of Vector{T} and Matrix{T} which allows functions to accept
either a Matrix or a Vector.

Examples
≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡

julia> Vector{Float64} <: VecOrMat{Float64}
true

julia> Matrix{Float64} <: VecOrMat{Float64}
true

julia> Array{Float64, 3} <: VecOrMat{Float64}
false

istaskdone(t::Task) -> Bool

Determine whether a task has exited.

Examples
≡≡≡≡≡≡≡≡

julia> a2() = sum(i for i in 1:1000);

julia> b = Task(a2);

julia> istaskdone(b)
false

julia> schedule(b);

julia> yield();

julia> istaskdone(b)
true

